course: everyday
lesson: 501
title: Big brothers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Big brothers often tease, and a tiny bit of praise from them can feel like a medal. In Cork, Cian, 14, lets Tadhg, 9, play FIFA with him for the first time. Tadhg scores and Cian says, «Not bad, ya sap» («sap» is Irish slang for a softie or a fool, said fondly here). Tadhg is chuffed (very pleased) for a week. In Belfast, a gang of older kids tries to take Eoin's football; his big brother Conor, 15, walks up and says, «Is there a problem, lads?» They leave. Eoin feels safe and proud. Look for the small kindness under the teasing, and say thanks.
explain: But teasing that hurts every day can turn into bullying, even from a brother. In Sydney, Lucas, 13, calls Mateus, 9, «short stuff», «titch» and «ya little grub» all day long. Mateus laughs along, then cries in his room. A joke is only a joke if both people are laughing. Mateus should tell his parents: they need to know it isn't just a joke for him.
explain: Teenage brothers need space. In Philadelphia, Malik, 16, has friends round, and Dante, 10, tries to hang with them. «Bro, go upstairs, you're doing too much» («doing too much» = being over the top). Dante slams his door. It isn't hate: teens want time with their own friends. In Calgary, Lorcan, 16, stops talking, stays in his room and grunts at dinner. Jaxon asks, «What's up with him, eh?» Mom says, «Teenager, bud.» Going quiet is common and usually passes, but if a brother seems really sad for a long time, tell a parent.
explain: Being someone's little brother can follow you around. In Merthyr Tydfil, teachers say to Rhys, «Oh, you're Gethin's brother, are you? Let's hope you're different.» Rhys feels judged before he's done anything. In Brighton, everyone asks Benji, «Are you going to swim like Ash?», but Benji hates swimming. You are your own person: you can ask a teacher for a fresh start, and find what you love. In London, Kojo, 16, teaches Kwame some bare (lots of) rude words for a laugh; Kwame says one in class. «Kwame, OUT,» says Miss. «My brother said it» doesn't get you out of trouble: think before you copy.
explain: Some changes are big. In Inverness, Torquil, 18, has joined the army, and his bedroom is empty. Finlay goes in and lies on his bed, missing him loads: «It's pure quiet without him» («pure» is Scots for really). Writing, video calls and telling Mum you miss him all help. In Plymouth, Koa gets a new bike and Ollie-Joe gets Koa's old one. «Proper job, it's like new,» says Dad (West Country for «excellent»). Ollie-Joe is annoyed, then adds stickers and a new bell and loves it. Hand-me-downs can be made your own.
fact: Big brothers often tease, but their small praise can mean a lot to a younger sibling.
fact: Many big brothers protect younger ones when trouble comes.
fact: Teasing that hurts every day can become bullying, even from a brother, and parents need to know.
fact: A joke is only a joke if both people are laughing.
fact: Teenagers often want space from younger siblings, especially around their friends; it is not hate.
fact: Teenagers often go quiet for a while; it usually passes, but if a sibling seems really sad for a long time, tell a parent.
fact: Teachers sometimes compare siblings, which is unfair; you can ask for a fresh start.
fact: You don't have to follow in a brother's footsteps; find what you love.
fact: Copying something rude a brother taught you still gets you in trouble; «my brother said it» is no excuse.
fact: Missing a brother who has left home is natural; calls, letters and talking help.
fact: Younger kids often get hand-me-downs, and these can be made your own.
fact: «Sap» is Irish slang for a softie, «chuffed» means very pleased, «doing too much» means being over the top, «bare» is London slang for lots, «pure» is Scots for really, and «proper job» is West Country for excellent.
exercise: Cian says «Not bad, ya sap» after Tadhg scores. How does Tadhg feel? | Chuffed for a week | Hurt and angry at being called a sap | Bored of FIFA | Scared of Cian
exercise: What does «chuffed» mean? | Very pleased | Out of breath | Very cross | A bit sick
exercise: What did Conor do when older kids tried to take Eoin's ball? | Asked «Is there a problem, lads?» and they left | Started a big fight with every one of the kids | Ran home to get their mum to come and help | Told Eoin to let them keep the ball this time, to be fair
exercise: Lucas calls Mateus «short stuff» and «little grub» every day. Mateus laughs, then cries in his room. What is it becoming? | Bullying | Normal fun | A good nickname | A game they both like
exercise: What should Mateus do? | Tell his parents it isn't a joke for him | Laugh louder so Lucas stops | Call Lucas even worse names back every day | Stay in his room for ever
exercise: When is a joke really a joke? | When both people are laughing | When the person telling it laughs | When it's said with a grin | When grown-ups aren't there
exercise: Malik says, «Bro, go upstairs, you're doing too much.» Why? | He wants time with his own friends | He hates his little brother | Dante broke something of Malik's earlier | Their mum told him to say it
exercise: Lorcan, 16, stays in his room and grunts at dinner. What does Mom say? | «Teenager, bud» | «He's poorly, love» | «He hates us all now» | «He's leaving home»
exercise: When should Jaxon tell a parent about Lorcan? | If he seems really sad for a long time | Only if Lorcan stops grunting at tea | Never, it's none of Jaxon's business | Every time Lorcan closes his door
exercise: A teacher tells Rhys, «Let's hope you're different from Gethin.» What can Rhys do? | Ask for a fresh start | Behave like Gethin so no one's disappointed | Change schools | Say nothing ever
exercise: Benji hates swimming, but his brother Ash is a champion. What does the lesson say? | Find what you love; you don't have to follow him | He must swim, because it runs in the family | He should quit everything to avoid comparison | He should train twice as hard every morning until he beats Ash
exercise: Kwame said a rude word his brother taught him. Does «my brother said it» get him off? | No | Yes | Only if Kojo comes in | Only on Fridays
exercise: Kojo taught Kwame «bare» rude words. What does «bare» mean in London slang? | Lots of | No | Old-fashioned | Bear
exercise: Torquil has joined the army. What can help Finlay? | Writing, video calls and telling Mum he misses him | Moving into Torquil's room and throwing his stuff out | Pretending he never had a big brother at all | Joining the army himself at ten years old
exercise: Ollie-Joe gets Koa's old bike. How does he make it his own? | Stickers and a new bell | Paints Koa's name on it | Leaves it in the shed | Swaps it for sweets
exercise: «Proper job, it's like new.» What does «proper job» mean? | Excellent | A real job | Hard work | Too old
test: «my bro says I'm rubbish at FIFA every day but once he said 'not bad', lol». What does the lesson say? | Notice the small kindness under the teasing | Teasing means he totally hates you | Praise from a brother is always fake | Never play FIFA with him again, he'll only be mean
test: «it's only banter, bro, he laughs» — but Mateus cries later in his room. What's true? | It's not a joke if one person isn't really laughing | It's banter if anyone is laughing at that moment | Crying later doesn't count, only the laugh counts | Brothers are allowed to say anything they like at home
test: What would probably happen if Dante kept following Malik's friends around? | Malik would get more annoyed | They'd make Dante their leader | Malik would thank him for it | Nothing would ever change
test: «my brother doesn't talk any more, he must hate me». What's more likely? | Teenagers often go quiet for a while | He definitely hates his whole family | He has lost his voice for a year | He wants Jaxon to move out of the house
test: In a film, the little brother has to be a swimming star because his brother is. What does Benji's story say? | You can find your own thing | Little brothers must copy | Swimming is the only sport | Families should pick your hobbies for you
test: Kwame's mate says, «just tell Miss your brother taught you, you'll be sweet». Will it work? | No, he still said it | Yes, Miss will let him off | Yes, and Kojo gets the detention | No, because Miss hates brothers
test: «Finlay's being soft, lying on his brother's bed». What's true? | Missing a brother who has left is natural | Big boys should never miss anyone at all | He's trying to steal Torquil's bedroom | He should forget about Torquil quickly
test: «Ollie-Joe only got the old bike, that's well tight». How did it turn out? | He made it his own and loved it | He refused to ride it ever | Koa gave him the new bike | Dad bought him a better one
test: ★★★ Koa got a new bike at 9 and gave it to Ollie-Joe 5 years later. Ollie-Joe is 5 years younger than Koa. How old is Ollie-Joe when he gets it? | 9 | 14 | 5 | 4
test: ★★★ Cian, Tadhg and two friends play FIFA. Each player plays every other player once. How many games? | 6 | 4 | 8 | 12
test: Rhys's mate says, «You're Gethin's brother so you must be trouble too». What's the fair view? | You are your own person | Brothers are always the same | Rhys should prove it by being bad | Teachers are always right
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises teasing which causes distress on a daily basis? | It may constitute bullying, of which parents ought to be informed | It is harmless provided that the person teased laughs at the time | It is permitted without limit between brothers within the home | It is a form of affection which requires no attention whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason, as established in the lesson, for an adolescent's desire to exclude a younger sibling from his company. | A need for space amongst his own friends | A settled hatred towards the sibling | An instruction from the parents | A wish to punish the younger child
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning comparisons drawn by teachers between siblings. | Such comparisons are unfair, and a fresh start may be requested | Such comparisons are invariably accurate and must be accepted | A younger sibling is obliged to resemble the elder in all respects | A pupil may not raise such a matter with a member of staff
exam: It is given that a pupil repeated in class an improper expression taught to him by an elder brother. Ascertain whether the brother's instruction excuses the pupil. | It does not excuse him | It wholly excuses him | It excuses him in part | It transfers the fault entirely to the brother
